    "passage": "Sealing the Bering Strait: A Drastic Solution to Global Warming\n\nCould an enormous dam slow climate change? Two Dutch scientists think it might. They propose sealing off the Bering Strait, the roughly 80-kilometer waterway between Russia and Alaska, to protect a vital system of ocean currents known as the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation, or AMOC.\n\nThe AMOC works like a conveyor belt, carrying warm, salty water northwards and helping maintain Europe's mild climate. However, as Greenland's ice melts, fresh water is weakening the current and pushing it toward collapse. Such a collapse could raise sea levels, cool Europe, and trigger droughts.\n\nUsing computer models, the researchers found that blocking the strait would restore the balance of salt water and fresh water, thereby strengthening the AMOC. Their plan calls for three dams stretching across the gap. If built by 2050, the structures could help prevent disaster.\n\nStill, experts urge caution. Cutting off the strait might harm marine life and disrupt ocean patterns in ways nobody fully understands. Any project this large, they warn, demands careful study.\n\nTaiwan Travelogue Wins the International Booker Prize\n\nTaiwanese writer Yang Shuang-zi has brought Taiwan's literature to the world stage with Taiwan Travelogue. In May 2026, the English version of the novel, translated by Lin King, won the International Booker Prize, one of the most prestigious awards for fiction translated into English. It is the first book translated from Mandarin Chinese to receive the honor.\n\nThe novel is set in Taiwan during the 1930s, when the island was under Japanese rule. It follows a fictional Japanese writer and her Taiwanese interpreter as they travel across Taiwan and explore its food, landscapes, and underlying tensions. Beneath its descriptions of local dishes and scenic routes, the story examines colonial power, cultural identity, memory, and love. Cleverly, the book presents itself as a rediscovered memoir, blurring the line between fiction and reality.\n\nThe award also highlights the essential role of translation. By bringing Yang's layered narrative into English, Lin King helped international readers encounter a complex Taiwan rarely seen in world literature. Many see the win as a milestone for Taiwanese voices.",
    "chineseTranslation": "封住白令海峽：對抗全球暖化的激進方案（80公里大壩，能擋住氣候災難嗎？）\n\n一座巨大的水壩能減緩氣候變遷嗎？兩位荷蘭科學家認為有可能。他們提議封住白令海峽——這條位於俄羅斯與阿拉斯加之間、寬約80公里的水道——以保護一個名為「大西洋經向翻轉環流」（AMOC）的重要洋流系統。\n\nAMOC 就像一條輸送帶，把溫暖、含鹽量高的海水往北輸送，幫助維持歐洲溫和的氣候。然而，隨著格陵蘭的冰層融化，淡水正在削弱這股洋流，將它推向崩潰。這樣的崩潰可能導致海平面上升、歐洲變冷，並引發乾旱。\n\n研究人員利用電腦模型發現，封住海峽能恢復鹹水與淡水的平衡，進而強化 AMOC。他們的計畫需要興建三座橫跨海峽的水壩。如果能在2050年前建成，這些結構物或許有助於避免災難。\n\n不過，專家呼籲要謹慎。截斷海峽可能傷害海洋生物，並以沒人能完全理解的方式擾亂海洋型態。他們警告，任何規模這麼大的工程，都需要審慎研究。\n\n《臺灣漫遊錄》抱回國際布克獎（《臺灣漫遊錄》走向世界，抱回布克獎）\n\n台灣作家楊双子以《臺灣漫遊錄》把台灣文學帶上了世界舞台。2026年5月，由金翎（Lin King）翻譯的英文版小說贏得國際布克獎，這是英譯小說最具聲望的獎項之一。這也是第一本從華文翻譯而獲得此殊榮的作品。\n\n這部小說的背景設定在1930年代日治時期的台灣，描寫一位虛構的日本女作家和她的台灣女口譯員一起環島旅行，探索台灣的美食、風景與底層的緊張關係。在地方菜餚和風景路線的描寫之下，故事探討了殖民權力、文化認同、記憶與愛。巧妙的是，這本書以一本「重新被發現的回憶錄」的形式呈現，模糊了虛構與現實的界線。\n\n這個獎項也凸顯了翻譯的重要角色。金翎把楊双子層次豐富的敘事帶進英文，讓國際讀者得以認識一個在世界文學中罕見的、複雜的台灣。許多人把這次獲獎視為台灣聲音的一個里程碑。",
